 we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x=\\dfrac 1 2 \\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n${\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 1 \\\\ \\hline u & - 3 & 3 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{1}\\sinh (2x + 1)dx &= \\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh udu \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}\\cosh u\\right|_{-3}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh (-3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh 3) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166623 :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\csch(3x+1)\\coth(3x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186166624 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} {\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}}\\, \\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186166624 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3$. Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d} u = x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n${\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 2 \\\\ \\hline u & - 8 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}\\, \\textrm{d}x &=\\int_{-2}^{2} \\sinh{x^3}\\cdot x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\int_{-8}^{8} \\sinh{u}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &=\\left.\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cosh u\\Big\\right|_{-8}^{8} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{(-8)}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{8}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166625 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186166629 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$"] [17592186166630 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x.$"] [17592186166631 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x   \\, \\textrm{d}x  }.$"] [17592186166633 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\dfrac{1}{1+\\sinh^2 x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166634 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 2} \\dfrac{\\tanh{x}-\\tanh^3{x}}{\\sech{x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166635 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\csch{x}}{\\coth^2{x} - 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166636 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\cosh^2(2x+1)-1} \\, \\textrm{d}x}.$"] [17592186166640 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\int 4\\sinh 2x \\cosh 2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166641 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\cosh^2{x} + \\sinh^2{x}\\ \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166642 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2\\cosh^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166643 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 2} 8\\sinh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166644 :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\textrm{d}x= }$"] [17592186166645 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(2x-2)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186166646 :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{6}{|3x+9|\\sqrt{x^2+6x+10}} \\textrm{d}x= }$"] [17592186166647 :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{3-2x-x^2} \\textrm{d}x=$"] [17592186166651 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$"] [17592186166695 :question/problem "Given that the moment-generating function (MGF) of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{1}{4} e^t \\right)^2,$\n\ncompute $\\textrm E[X^k]$, where $k$ is a positive integer."] [17592186166716 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186166717 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186166717 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186166722 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\sech{x}$?\n\n1. The function is increasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n2. The function has a vertical asymptote.\n3. The domain of the function is $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, \\infty)$."] [17592186166723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\csch{x}$?\n\n1. The function has no zeros.\n2. $\\csch{x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^-$.\n3. The function is even."] [17592186166724 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\coth{x}$?\n\n1. The function has a zero at $x=0$.\n2. $\\coth{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n3. The range of the function is $(0, \\infty)$."] [17592186167064 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} + \\cos ty^{″} - t^{2}y^{′} + y = 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$."] [17592186167072 :question/problem "The third-order initial value problem\n$y^{‴} - y = 0, y(2) = 1, y^{′}(2) =-2, y^{″}(2) =-3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{1} ( 2 ) = p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{2} ( 2 ) = q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t ) , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{3} ( 2 ) = r\\end{array} \\right.$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$, and $p$, $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q - r) \\cdot f(t)$."] [17592186167073 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation $y^{‴} + 7\\sqrt{t}y^{′} + 10y = 0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where\n\n$x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime\\prime } = {x}_{3} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$."] [17592186167109 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{e^{-3x^{2}}}{x^{3} + x}\\right)$ at $x = 0.5$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167123 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{\\sin x - x}{x^{2} - x\\cos x}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is decreasing on $x ∈ (-2,-1)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-2) \\approx - 0.255$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}f(x)dx \\approx 0.217$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167124 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\frac{xe^{\\sin x} + 1}{x}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is increasing on $x ∈ (-4,-2)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-3) \\approx - 0.971$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^{-2}f(x)dx \\approx 1.241$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167138 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167139 :question/problem "If\n$u(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186167158 :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186167162 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - {x}_{1} ( t ) + 3 {x}_{2} ( t ) ,\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system $X_{1}(s) - 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhere $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$, respectively."] [17592186167168 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 3xy + 1, y\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) =-1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $y_{1}^{′}$ | $y_{2}^{′}$ | $y_{3}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(0) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186167171 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\sin (2t)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n$x_{c}(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t) = x_{c}(t) + x_{p}(t)$, where $x_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $x_{p}(t)$."] [17592186167172 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186167173 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+0\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186167176 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - y, y(0) = 3$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$, fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $3$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |"] [17592186167177 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n$y^{′} = f(x, y), y(a) = c$,\nand we take a step of size $Δx$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Fill in the blanks to complete the weighted-average formula for the increment and the stage definitions, starting at the point $(x, y)$.$y^{′} + 2y_{1}^{′} + 2y_{2}^{′} + y_{3}^{′}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}"] [17592186167181 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8}+\\frac{37}{16})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{153}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{37}{16}$ | $\\frac{51}{64}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{51}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{115}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{37}{16}$ | $\\frac{51}{64}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{115}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{115}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167183 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x - y, y(0) = 2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} |"] [17592186167184 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation."] [17592186167187 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}_{1} ( t ) + 2 {t}^{2} + 1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - {x}_{1} ( t ) + 3 {x}_{2} ( t ) - 3 ,\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system$sX_{1}(s) - 2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhere $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$, respectively."] [17592186167190 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n$y^{′} = f(x, y), y(1) = 0$\nfor some function $f(x, y)$. The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $y_{1}^{′}$ | $y_{2}^{′}$ | $y_{3}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx = 1$, at which of the following points is the function $f$ **NOT** evaluated during this iteration?"] [17592186167192 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x - 2y + 1, y(1) = 2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $y_{1}^{′}$ | $y_{2}^{′}$ | $y_{3}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $$? |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167196 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167197 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"] [17592186167199 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n$Δy|= y^{′} \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nIf the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = x^{2}$, find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_{new} = x + Δx$."] [17592186167200 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)"] [17592186167201 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2y, y(0) = 1$\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n$Δy|= y^{′} \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = e^{2x}$, compute the local truncation error $τ_{1}$ for the first step starting at $x = 0$ with step size $Δx = 0.1$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$τ_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167202 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x + 1, y(0) = 0$\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n$Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(x + y^{′}) \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + x$, compute the local truncation error, $τ_{1}$, for the first step starting at $x = 0$ with step size $Δx = 0.2$.$τ_{1} =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167204 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 3x + 2y, y(0) = 1$\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186167205 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x^{2} + 2xy + y^{2}, y(0) = 1$\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186167206 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 4 {x}_{1} ( t ) - {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 {x}_{1} ( t ) + 3 {x}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system$sX_{1}(s) + 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhere $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$, respectively."] [17592186167207 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/f5/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"] [17592186167209 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + 2 y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} = 4$ and $λ_{2} = 1$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$e^{-t}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [17592186167210 :question/problem "![](images/83/83885d091455a8f9b9a1c33556d9deeaee550d50dd665e016c2e8072c926f41a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1}$ and $λ_{2}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [1, 2]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [2, 1]^{T}$, respectively.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$-\\frac{1}{2}x$ {{field-1}}\nThe tangents along this line are pointing\n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form\n$c_{1}e^{λ_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach\n{{field-3}}\nas $t → ∞$. So, we must have\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186167211 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/e0/e0446b7851fe0f0612d3db67de05188c0ed63af41ff1dfe2773158b2382fe722.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line\n\nSince ${e}^{t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa12152c9882d0953667f26e888d0fbac375f4cc0c348f3710799e1a6e22349.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/83/83885d091455a8f9b9a1c33556d9deeaee550d50dd665e016c2e8072c926f41a.png)"] [17592186167211 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/e0/e0446b7851fe0f0612d3db67de05188c0ed63af41ff1dfe2773158b2382fe722.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa12152c9882d0953667f26e888d0fbac375f4cc0c348f3710799e1a6e22349.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/83/83885d091455a8f9b9a1c33556d9deeaee550d50dd665e016c2e8072c926f41a.png)"] [17592186167212 :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f5dc595e9c7efe6a844bfb80449107392ce780ed6f00697bbba512a91dc691d.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1}$ and $λ_{2}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$, respectively.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$-\\frac{1}{2}x$ {{field-1}}\nThe tangents along this line are pointing\n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form\n$c_{1}e^{λ_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach\n{{field-3}}\nas $t → ∞$. So, we must have\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186167213 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) - y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 4 x ( t ) - 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-1$ and $λ_{2} = 2$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [1, 4]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [1, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n$y = 4x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}."] [17592186167214 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represents the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/52/52f6032c8aa3fd791b80fec42ffb46941408e871435a208573441deb087dffdf.png)"] [17592186167215 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)"] [17592186167216 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - x ( t ) + 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186167217 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186167218 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[2,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)"] [17592186167218 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)"] [17592186167220 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 2 x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v = [1, 0]^{T}$. The vector $w = [0, 1]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$e^{2t}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167222 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}_{1} ( t ) + 3 {e}^{2 t} + 1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}_{1} ( t ) + {x}_{2} ( t ) - 4 ,\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system$X_{1}(s) - 2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhere $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$, respectively."] [17592186167223 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 4 y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 9 x ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = ± 6\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± 2\\textrm{i}, 3]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sin (6t) \\\\ 3\\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (6t) \\\\ 3\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$3\\sin (6t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167224 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 x ( t ) - 3 y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = 2 ± 3\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± \\textrm{i}, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$e^{2t}\\sin (3t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167225 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{y}^{\\prime } = {\\displaystyle \\frac{x y}{z}} + 1 , \\hspace{1em} & y ( - 1 ) = - 2 \\\\ {z}^{\\prime } = x + y z - 1 , \\hspace{1em} & z ( - 1 ) = 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0)$.$y(0) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$z(0) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186167227 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{y}^{\\prime } = 3 x z + 2 y , \\hspace{1em} & y ( 1 ) = 1 \\\\ {z}^{\\prime } = {\\displaystyle \\frac{z}{( x + y {)}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & z ( 1 ) = - 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $z(2)$.$y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$z(2) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186167231 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w = w(x)$:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{y}^{\\prime } = x + 3 y - z , \\hspace{1em} & y ( 0 ) = 1 \\\\ {z}^{\\prime } = x - w + 2 z , \\hspace{1em} & z ( 0 ) = 1 \\\\ {w}^{\\prime } = w y + x z , \\hspace{1em} & w ( 0 ) = - 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$, $Δz$, and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | $w^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-4}},\n$z(1) \\approx$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w(1) \\approx$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186167235 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2196ea04e12f3c706300e0c9d15806606e408f5e582d3e4f2b9c62f0efc343f.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/14/1429eda4336d47aec06c7dd673ff6163203423739c520fd0c83db1c5cb072ac7.png)"] [17592186167236 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/19/19cd34ffc84e4a904d87cdfe0d8b6721861a0a39025484946dbe471a5ea4ad87.png)"] [17592186167237 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/2a/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png)"] [17592186167238 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ 18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186167239 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/63/6337a670b6fa73c4c21759b600269cb83d808a1c13026c90e2d02519fa31cd67.png)"] [17592186167246 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x-3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-3xy)=2-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y & -3x \\\\ 4y & -5+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-3(0) & -3(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -5+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [17592186167247 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = x - 0.5 x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - 0.2 y + 2 x y\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium $x^{∗} = [0.1, 2]^{T}$, we get\n\n| $J(x^{∗}) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186167249 :question/problem "$\\text{I.} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = 3 x - x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = 5 x y\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\text{II.} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = x - 2 x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - 5 y + 0.1 x y\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\text{III.} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = 0.5 x - 3 y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - y + 2 x\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhich of the above are Lotka-Volterra predator-prey models for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$?"] [17592186167252 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = 2 x ( 1 - x ) - x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - 2 y + 4 x y\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium $x^{∗} = [1, 0]^{T}$, we get\n\n| $J(x^{∗}) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186167253 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = 2 x ( 1 - 4 x ) - x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - 2 y + 4 x y\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium $x^{∗} = [0.25, 0]^{T}$, we get\n\n| $J(x^{∗}) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186167254 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-4x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=2-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-16x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-16(0)-(0) & -(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -2+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [17592186167258 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w = w(x)$:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{y}^{\\prime } = 1 + x + z - y , \\hspace{1em} & y ( 0 ) = 1 \\\\ {z}^{\\prime } = 2 - x + y - w , \\hspace{1em} & z ( 0 ) = 1 \\\\ {w}^{\\prime } = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 x + y}{2 z w}} , \\hspace{1em} & w ( 0 ) = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$, $z(1)$, and $w(1)$.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$z(1) \\approx$\n{{field-2}},\n$w(1) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186167259 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } = 3 x ( 1 - 0.2 x ) - x y \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = - 4 y + 1.5 x y\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.The maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186167262 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-4}} matrix."] [17592186167269 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"] [17592186167272 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x + 2y, y(-1) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = 1$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $y_{1}^{′}$ | $y_{2}^{′}$ | $y_{3}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167273 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 8y, y(1) = \\frac{1}{2}$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $y_{1}^{′}$ | $y_{2}^{′}$ | $y_{3}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167276 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos (3x)\\cos (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186167277 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos (5x)\\cos (x)dx$?"] [17592186167278 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167279 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186167280 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167281 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186167302 :question/problem "$x_{1} = 4, x_{2} =-2, x_{3} = 7, x_{4} =-5, x_{5} = 1$\nGiven the values $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}, x_{5}$ above, we have$∑_(i = 1)^(5)1[x_{i} > 0] \\cdot x_{i} =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186167305 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x+1) \\\\  & =1(-1+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & ={y}_{1}({x}_{1}+1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={y}_{2}({x}_{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ =(-1+1,\\,1+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\ =(0,\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & ={y}_{3}({x}_{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}\\cdot (0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{13}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{31}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{31}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{79}{48}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈\\frac{79}{48}.$"] [17592186167306 :question/problem "Consider the polynomials $p_{1}(t)$ and $p_{2}(t)$, given by\n$p_{1}(t) = 1 + t, p_{2}(t) = 1 - t^{2}$.\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B = {p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $Span{p_{1}, p_{2}}$, and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186167307 :question/problem "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186167308 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$p_{1}(t) = 1 + t^{2}, p_{2}(t) = 1 - t + 2t^{2}, q(t) = 1 - 3t + 4t^{2}$.\n\nIf $B = {p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $Span{p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t)}$, and $[q(t)]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $x_{1} + x_{2} =$"] [17592186167309 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1} & 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1} &=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.$"] [17592186167310 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n$p_{1}(t) = t^{3} + t + 1, p_{2}(t) = t^{3} + t^{2} - 2t + 1, p_{3}(t) = 2t^{2} - 4t + 2, q(t) = t^{3} + t^{2} - 3t$.\n\nIf $B = {p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)}$ is a basis of $Span{p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)}$, and $[q(t)]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $x_{1} =$"] [17592186167314 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [17592186167330 :question/problem "Consider the following matric