: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-20I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186167433 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4-(2x-2)^{2}}}.$"] [17592186167434 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|6x-2|\\sqrt{9+(3x-1)^{2}}}.$"] [17592186167435 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167436 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|4x-8|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167437 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{9-(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167438 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{1-4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186167439 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{3}y=5{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}?$"] [17592186167447 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 6x + 13}}$?"] [17592186167449 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{16x^{2} + 32x}}$?"] [17592186167462 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{3/10}^{2/5}\\frac{2}{1 - 25x^{2}}dx$."] [17592186167463 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186167518 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$"]}
